          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Ice Melt Actually Damages Concrete
          </h2>

          <p>
            The main way de-icers damage concrete is indirect. Concrete is porous. When ice melts on the surface, the water and brine soak into the top layer of the slab. When temperatures drop again, that water freezes and expands by about 9 percent, putting pressure on the surrounding cement paste. Repeat that cycle dozens of times in a winter and the top layer begins to flake and pop off, a problem called scaling. De-icers increase the number of freeze-thaw cycles at the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Salt also adds stress on its own. Brine soaked into concrete creates layers with different salt concentrations, which freeze at different temperatures and produce uneven internal pressure. Salt crystals forming in the pores as brine dries can add pressure too. This is why scaling is often worst near the edges of driveways and walks, where brine collects and sits, and in low spots where melt pools.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some chemicals add direct attack. Research from transportation agencies and universities has linked calcium chloride and magnesium chloride to chemical reactions with the cement paste, including the formation of calcium oxychloride, an expansive compound that can damage concrete even without freeze-thaw cycles. Magnesium can also degrade the binding compounds in the paste. Rock salt is less chemically aggressive than those two but still drives freeze-thaw scaling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete quality decides how much any of this matters. Properly air-entrained concrete, finished correctly and cured for weeks, has millions of tiny air bubbles that give freezing water room to expand. It can tolerate de-icers far better than a slab that was overwatered, overworked during finishing, or poured without air entrainment. Unfortunately, many residential slabs fall into that second group, which is why choosing a gentler product is worth the effort.
          </p>

