        <div id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. De-Icer Chemistry Compared: Melting Power vs Concrete Risk
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Sodium chloride</strong>, ordinary rock salt, is cheap and effective down to roughly 15 to 20 degrees Fahrenheit in practice. It causes freeze-thaw scaling and corrodes metal and rebar, but is less chemically aggressive toward cement paste than calcium or magnesium chloride. <strong>Calcium chloride</strong> works far colder, well below zero, and melts fast, but research associates it with chemical damage to concrete, and it is harsh on skin and paws.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Magnesium chloride</strong> sits between them in cold performance, and it is often marketed as a safer alternative. It is gentler on plants than calcium chloride, but magnesium compounds can attack the cement paste, so its reputation as concrete-friendly is not well supported. <strong>Potassium chloride</strong> is gentler on concrete and plants but only works near the freezing point, around 25 degrees Fahrenheit, so it is usually blended with other salts.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Calcium magnesium acetate (CMA)</strong> is chloride-free, far less corrosive, and widely considered the gentlest common de-icer for concrete and steel. Its downsides are cost and limited cold performance, roughly 20 degrees Fahrenheit and above. <strong>Urea and amide-based</strong> products, common in pet-safe melters, are gentle on concrete and metal but also weak in deep cold, and urea in large amounts adds nitrogen to runoff.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most products on store shelves are blends designed to balance speed, temperature range, cost and gentleness. Reading the ingredients is more useful than reading the marketing. A bag that says concrete safe but lists calcium or magnesium chloride as the main ingredient is making a relative claim, not an absolute one. For a broader look at protecting the slab itself, see our <a href="/best-concrete-driveway-sealer-reviews/">concrete driveway sealer reviews</a>.
          </p>

