        <div id="avoid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Products to Avoid on Concrete
          </h2>

          <p>
            Never use ammonium nitrate or ammonium sulfate on concrete. These fertilizers do melt ice, and some people grab them from the garage in a pinch, but ammonium compounds chemically attack concrete and can cause severe deterioration quickly. Cement and concrete industry guidance specifically warns against them. If a bag of fertilizer is the only thing you have, use sand for traction instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be cautious with straight calcium chloride and magnesium chloride on anything but older, well-built concrete in good condition. Avoid them entirely on new slabs, stamped or stained decorative concrete, and concrete that is already scaling, where any extra stress accelerates damage. When you do use them, use small amounts and shovel the brine away quickly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip homemade mixes involving dish soap, rubbing alcohol and hot water for anything larger than a step or two. They work in small doses, but pouring hot water on frozen concrete creates a thermal shock and more freeze-thaw cycling, and the runoff refreezes as a thin glaze. They are a stopgap, not a winter strategy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, be wary of vague labels. Phrases like safe for concrete or won't harm concrete are marketing, and they usually mean less harmful than rock salt under normal use. Look for the ingredient list, the stated working temperature, and any fine print about new concrete. Many reputable brands warn against use on concrete under a year old even when they advertise concrete safety.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="new-concrete" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The First-Winter Rule for New Concrete
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete poured in the past year is the most vulnerable to de-icers. It is still gaining strength, it holds more internal moisture, and it has not yet built up resistance at the surface. Concrete industry guidance commonly recommends that no de-icing chemicals be used during the first winter, and some contractors suggest avoiding them for the first two winters, especially if the slab was poured late in the season.
          </p>

          <p>
            That means relying on shoveling and traction. Clear snow promptly before it gets packed down. For ice, spread clean sand, chicken grit, or non-clumping traction material for grip. Sand does not melt anything, but it keeps people upright without putting chemicals into the young slab. Sweep it up in spring so it does not get tracked indoors or into drains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch out for indirect exposure. Cars bring salt brine from city streets and drip it onto a new driveway all winter. Rinse the slab during thaws when the temperature allows, and consider a garage mat if the new concrete is in the garage. Neighbors and snow-removal services may also throw salt on your walk without thinking about it, so ask them not to.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealing helps, but timing matters. New concrete needs to cure, typically at least 28 days, before a penetrating sealer can be applied, and sealer makers set their own minimums. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer applied before the first winter can meaningfully reduce water and salt absorption. Our <a href="/best-penetrating-concrete-sealer/">penetrating concrete sealer guide</a> covers the products made for that job, and <a href="/how-to-seal-a-concrete-driveway/">how to seal a concrete driveway</a> walks through the steps.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pavers-sealed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Ice Melt on Pavers, Stamped and Sealed Surfaces
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete pavers are made from dense, high-strength concrete and are generally more resistant to de-icers than poured slabs, which is one reason they are popular in cold climates. Many paver manufacturers allow the use of common de-icers but advise against excessive amounts and against products containing ammonium compounds. Rock salt or a blend used in moderation is usually acceptable on pavers, but check your manufacturer's guidance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Joint sand is the weak point. Heavy brine and repeated shoveling can loosen and wash out joint sand, and some de-icers may affect polymeric sand binders. Shovel carefully along joints, use a plastic blade where possible, and check the joints in spring. Our <a href="/best-polymeric-sand-for-pavers/">polymeric sand guide</a> covers which sands hold up best in freeze-thaw climates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealed surfaces deserve extra care. Acrylic sealers on stamped, stained or paver surfaces can be softened or whitened by some de-icers, and metal shovel blades scrape them. A penetrating sealer is generally more tolerant of winter conditions than an acrylic film. If you have a sealed patio or stamped driveway, gentler products like CMA or pet-safe melters are worth the extra cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are choosing a sealer with winter in mind, look at penetrating silane-siloxane products for areas that see snow and salt. Our <a href="/best-paver-sealer/">paver sealer guide</a> explains which chemistries hold up best, and the <a href="/best-water-based-paver-sealer-reviews/">water-based paver sealer roundup</a> includes penetrating options suited to cold climates.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Much to Use and When to Apply It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people use far more ice melt than they need. Labels typically call for a few ounces per square yard, which looks sparse, with granules spaced an inch or two apart. More product does not melt faster in proportion; it mostly creates more brine to soak into the concrete. A handheld or walk-behind spreader gives a far more even spread than tossing handfuls, and it cuts product use noticeably.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing matters as much as amount. Applying a small amount before or at the very start of a storm keeps ice from bonding to the surface, so snow can be shoveled cleanly. This anti-icing approach uses less chemical than trying to melt thick ice afterward. If the storm has already passed, shovel first, then apply melt only to the ice that remains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remove the slush once the ice breaks. This is the step that does the most to protect concrete. Leaving brine and slush on the surface lets it soak in and refreeze overnight. Shovel or push it off onto areas where it will not harm plants, ideally not onto lawn edges or garden beds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Match the product to the temperature. Using rock salt at 5 degrees Fahrenheit wastes product because it barely works, and people tend to pile more on. Check the forecast and the working range on the bag. In mild winter weather, gentler products like CMA or pet-safe melters work fine; save the calcium chloride for the deep-cold mornings on older concrete.
          </p>

