        <div id="liquid-granular" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Liquid vs Granular Ice Melt
          </h2>

          <p>
            Granular products, pellets, crystals and flakes, are what most homeowners use. They are easy to store, easy to spread, and work on existing ice by boring down through it. Their weakness is uneven distribution: granules land in clusters, bounce off into lawns, and create concentrated pockets of brine. Pellets also get tracked into the house and can scratch floors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Liquid de-icers are brines sprayed on the surface. Their best use is anti-icing: sprayed on dry pavement before a storm, they leave a thin film that keeps snow and ice from bonding. They spread evenly, use far less chemical per square foot, and start working immediately. They are less effective at cutting through thick existing ice, and in very cold conditions the brine can refreeze if applied too heavily.
          </p>

          <p>
            For concrete, liquids have a real advantage: control. A measured, even film puts down less total chemical and avoids the pockets of concentrated brine that granules create. That matters more than the chemistry for many homeowners. The catch is that most liquid de-icers are chloride brines, so the same rules apply about new and decorative concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical combination is liquid pre-treatment before forecast storms, prompt shoveling, and a gentle granular product for spot-treating whatever ice remains. Dedicate a pump sprayer to the liquid de-icer and rinse it afterward, since salt brine corrodes metal parts. Do not use the same sprayer you use for sealers or garden chemicals.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pets-plants" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Pets, Plants and Indoor Floors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Chloride salts are hard on paws. They dry out and crack pads, and pets that lick their feet afterward can get an upset stomach, or worse with large amounts. Calcium chloride is the harshest because it releases heat as it dissolves. If pets use a walk, choose a pet-focused product and wipe paws when they come inside. Booties work for dogs that tolerate them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plants suffer from chlorides too. Salt-laden slush thrown onto lawns and beds damages roots and soil structure, and the damage often shows up in spring as browned grass edges and struggling shrubs. Pile snow away from beds where possible, use gentler products along garden edges, and water the soil deeply in spring to flush salt below the root zone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Indoors, de-icer residue leaves white films on hardwood, tile and carpet, and grit scratches finishes. Calcium and magnesium chloride residues can be oily and hard to clean. A coarse outdoor mat and an absorbent indoor mat at every entry catch most of it. Clean residue promptly with a damp mop and a cleaner made for salt residue.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pet-safe does not mean edible. Even products marketed as safe for pets should not be eaten in quantity, and the label usually says so. Store bags out of reach, sweep up leftover pellets once the ice is gone, and call your veterinarian if a pet eats a large amount of any de-icer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. What a Winter of Ice Melt Really Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Price per bag is misleading because the products are used at very different rates. Rock salt and mined chloride products are the cheapest per pound, often well under a dollar a pound in 50-pound bags at the time of writing. Calcium chloride pellets cost more per pound but are applied more sparingly. Pet-safe and acetate products commonly run several dollars a pound, and pure CMA is usually the most expensive option on a per-pound basis.
          </p>

          <p>
            To estimate a season, measure the areas you actually treat. A typical two-car driveway, front walk and steps add up to roughly 800 to 1,000 square feet. At label rates, a moderate winter with a dozen icing events might use 50 to 150 pounds of a chloride product, less if you pre-treat and shovel diligently, more if you broadcast heavily. Specialty products used at similar rates cost several times as much over the season.
          </p>

          <p>
            A split strategy keeps costs reasonable. Use the gentle, expensive product where it matters most: the front steps, the walk the dog uses, stamped or stained patios, and any concrete less than a couple of years old. Use a moderately priced blend on the older driveway, and keep a small bag of calcium chloride for the handful of deep-cold mornings each year. That way you protect the vulnerable surfaces without paying premium prices for every square foot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weigh the cost of damage, too. Replacing a scaled concrete walk or resurfacing a driveway costs thousands of dollars, far more than a decade of premium ice melt. A few extra dollars a season for gentler products on vulnerable surfaces, plus a penetrating sealer every several years, is cheap insurance for concrete you expect to last thirty years or more.
          </p>

