        <div id="protect" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Protecting Concrete Through Every Winter
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best long-term protection is a penetrating sealer. Silane and siloxane sealers soak into concrete and make it repel water and brine, which reduces both freeze-thaw scaling and chloride absorption. Apply in fall, on dry concrete, with temperatures within the label range, typically every several years. Our <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealer guide</a> compares the main options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fix drainage. Low spots where melt pools are where scaling starts. Redirect downspouts away from walks and driveways, and keep snow piles from draining across the slab. Patches of standing water that freeze overnight do more damage than the de-icer itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use plastic shovel blades on decorative and sealed surfaces, and set snowblower skids so the auger does not scrape the slab. Metal edges gouge sealers and chip the surface of weakened concrete, opening more pathways for water to enter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, rinse in spring. Once temperatures stay above freezing, hose off driveways, walks and patios to remove leftover salt residue, and flush garden beds next to them. A quick spring rinse removes the chloride that would otherwise keep working into the surface through the next season.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE WINTER RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Shovel more, melt less, and never leave brine sitting overnight.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The product you choose matters, but how you use it matters more. A gentle product piled on and left as slush can do more harm than a modest amount of rock salt that is spread thinly and shoveled off promptly.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Salting a brand-new slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concrete poured within the last year is the most vulnerable to scaling. Even products advertised as concrete safe often warn against use on new concrete in the fine print. Shovel promptly and use sand or grit for traction during the first winter, and rinse off any salt brine tracked in by cars.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Piling on extra product</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Doubling the dose does not double the melting. It mostly creates more brine to soak into the slab and refreeze. Use a spreader, follow the label rate, and expect granules to look widely spaced. Pre-treating before a storm uses far less product than chasing thick ice afterward.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the wrong melt for the temperature</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rock salt slows dramatically below about 15 degrees Fahrenheit, so people pour on more and get little result. Check the forecast and the working range on the bag. Use stronger melt only on the coldest days, and gentler products in mild weather. Rock salt piled on a subzero morning just becomes more brine later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving slush on the slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Melted slush sitting on concrete soaks in and refreezes overnight, which is exactly what drives scaling. Once the ice breaks up, shovel or push the slush off the surface. This one habit protects concrete more than switching brands of ice melt does. Push it toward a spot where it will not drain back across the concrete.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Grabbing fertilizer as a de-icer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ammonium nitrate and ammonium sulfate fertilizers melt ice, but ammonium compounds attack concrete chemically and can cause rapid deterioration. If you run out of ice melt, use sand, grit or non-clumping cat litter for traction until you can buy a proper product. Keep one spare bag of gentle melt on hand before the season starts.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

