        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which de-icer is the gentlest on concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Calcium magnesium acetate, sold as CMA, is generally considered the gentlest common de-icer for concrete and steel. Pet-safe products based on urea or amides are also low risk. Both are slower and less effective in deep cold than chlorides. No de-icer is completely harmless, because any melt water can soak in and refreeze, so shoveling promptly still matters.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is rock salt or calcium chloride worse for concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Calcium chloride works in much colder weather, but research links it, along with magnesium chloride, to chemical damage in cement paste on top of freeze-thaw scaling. Rock salt mainly drives scaling and corrodes rebar. For older, sound concrete, either can be used sparingly. For new or decorative concrete, avoid both and use CMA, pet-safe melters, or sand.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should new concrete go without de-icers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Common guidance is to avoid de-icing chemicals during the first winter after the concrete is poured, and some contractors suggest waiting through two winters for late-season pours. Use shoveling and sand for traction in the meantime. Applying a penetrating sealer once the concrete has cured, typically at least 28 days, adds protection for later winters.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will de-icers hurt my pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Concrete pavers are dense and generally tolerate common de-icers in moderation better than poured slabs, though many paver makers advise against heavy use and ammonium-based products. The bigger issue is joint sand, which brine and shoveling can loosen. Clay pavers and natural stone vary widely, so check the manufacturer's guidance and lean toward gentler products on sealed surfaces.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How cold is too cold for ice melt to work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the chemistry. Rock salt slows dramatically below about 15 to 20 degrees Fahrenheit. CMA and urea products work best above about 20 degrees. Magnesium chloride works to roughly 0 to 5 degrees, and calcium chloride works well below zero. Check the working range on the bag and match the product to the forecast.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are liquid de-icers easier on concrete than pellets?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Liquid de-icers can be easier on concrete because they spread evenly and use less total chemical, especially when sprayed before a storm to prevent ice from bonding. They are less effective at cutting through thick existing ice. Most are chloride brines, so they still should not be used on new or decorative concrete, and they should not be over-applied.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I seal concrete to protect it from salt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for most exterior concrete in cold climates. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer reduces how much water and brine soaks into the slab, which slows scaling and chloride damage. Apply it in fall on dry, cured concrete within the temperature range on the label, and reapply every several years according to the manufacturer's guidance.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

